WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
Two light-driven lifts carry each electron from water to NADPH.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.240
The protons left behind make ATP.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.480
But the Calvin cycle needs more ATP than that.

4
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.440
How can a leaf make ATP alone?

5
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:21.233
Count per oxygen molecule released.

6
00:00:22.773 --> 00:00:27.373
Linear flow makes 2 NADPH and leaves 12 protons in the lumen.

7
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:32.753
The Calvin cycle spends 3 ATP for every 2 NADPH.

8
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:36.347
Those protons pay at ATP synthase.

9
00:00:37.627 --> 00:00:42.307
Its rotor has 14 subunits, each carrying one proton across per turn.

10
00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:45.367
One full turn makes 3 ATP.

11
00:00:46.707 --> 00:00:48.487
So 14 protons buy 3.

12
00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:53.417
Twelve protons turn the rotor only twelve fourteenths of the way.

13
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:58.677
That makes about 2.6 ATP, roughly 0.4 short.

14
00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:02.337
The gap rests on that 14-subunit rotor.

15
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.980
Closing the gap costs two more protons.

16
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.300
Cytochrome b6f pumps two for every electron it passes.

17
00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.220
Predict: where could one electron go, to add protons but no NADPH?

18
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:25.497
Back to cytochrome b6f.

19
00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:31.677
At ferredoxin, the electron turns around instead of reaching NADP+.

20
00:01:32.477 --> 00:01:36.297
It falls through b6f, which pumps its two protons.

21
00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:41.317
Then it returns to Photosystem I, and a photon lifts it again.

22
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:46.627
Here is the idea. ATP is paid in protons.

23
00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:51.107
NADPH is paid in electrons that reach the end.

24
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:55.647
So a loop that never reaches the end buys ATP alone.

25
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.970
The chloroplast mixes the two routes.

26
00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:03.770
About one electron per oxygen molecule goes round the loop.

27
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.590
Its two protons bring the total to 14: three ATP.

28
00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:13.630
The price is about 9 photons per oxygen, instead of 8.

29
00:02:14.683 --> 00:02:18.923
Arnon showed that isolated chloroplasts make ATP both ways.

30
00:02:20.123 --> 00:02:24.023
The loop uses only the second lift, one photon per electron.

31
00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:29.383
Alongside other balancing routes, it brings the output to 3 to 2.

32
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:33.557
So the answer to the opening question is a detour.

33
00:02:35.017 --> 00:02:37.897
Electrons that finish the climb make NADPH.

34
00:02:39.017 --> 00:02:41.557
Electrons that circle only pump protons.

35
00:02:42.617 --> 00:02:46.157
The chloroplast sets its ratio partly by how many circle.
